@page "/reset-view"
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web

@*
    The reset-view escape: the addressable, always-reachable way to forget
    everything the Explorer remembers about how you like the shell and where you
    were.

    It lives at its own lower-case route rather than as a control buried in the
    chrome for a reason. A user who needs it most is usually a user whose
    remembered state is the problem - a view pinned to a tenant they can no
    longer see, or a surface that no longer resolves - so the escape must not
    depend on that chrome rendering correctly. A typed or bookmarked address
    always works.

    Resetting is deliberately an explicit action rather than something that
    happens on arrival, so following a link cannot silently discard somebody's
    preferences. It clears only this identity's own UI preferences: no cluster
    data, no sign-in, and no other user's or cluster's remembered view, because
    the contract scopes every key.
*@

<PageTitle>Reset view</PageTitle>

<h1>Reset view</h1>

@if (_reset)
{
    <p role="status">
        The Explorer has forgotten your remembered view. It will start from its
        default the next time you open it.
    </p>
    <p>
        <a href="/">Back to the Explorer</a>
    </p>
}
else
{
    <p>
        The Explorer remembers a few things about how you like it and where you
        were last time. Resetting forgets all of them for the account and cluster
        you are connected to now, and affects nothing else - no cluster data, and
        no sign-in.
    </p>

    <p>What is remembered:</p>
    <ul>
        @foreach (var key in Preferences.Keys)
        {
            <li>@key.Description</li>
        }
    </ul>

    <p>
        <button type="button" disabled="@_busy" @onclick="_resetClicked">Reset view</button>
        <a href="/">Cancel</a>
    </p>
}
